.TH VLHE 1 "2026-10-05" "VLHE 1.0" "VLHE Manual"
.SH NAME
vlhe, vlhe.gtk \- control VLHE, the virtual sound, CD-ROM and MIDI hardware
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vlhe volume
.RB [ \-q | \-\-plain ]
.br
.B vlhe volume
.I N LEVEL
.br
.B vlhe volume
.I N
.BR mute | unmute
.br
.B vlhe apply
.RB [ \-n | \-\-dry\-run ]
.RB [ \-u | \-\-unload ]
.RB [ \-\-boot ]
.br
.B vlhe status
.br
.B vlhe repair
.br
.B vlhe setup
.PP
.B vlhe.gtk
.RB [ \-\-reset ]
.RB [ \-m
.IR PAGE ]
.RB [ \-T
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-V ]
.RB [ \-F
.IR FONT ]
.RI [ "GTK options" ]
.br
.B vlhe.gtk
.I subcommand
.RI [ arguments ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
VLHE (Virtual Legacy Hardware Emulation) gives a Linux 2.2 machine,
in software, the sound and CD hardware that period programs expect:
a mixer that lets many programs share one sound card
.RB ( vsound.o " and " vsoundd (8)),
virtual CD-ROM drives made from disc images, with CD audio, CD-TEXT
and CD+G
.RB ( vdisc.o " and " vdiscd (8)),
and a SoundFont MIDI synthesizer
.RB ( vmidi.o " and " vmidid (8)).
.PP
.B vlhe
is the command-line tool. It needs no X server and links no toolkit,
so it works on a console, over a serial line, and after a boot where
X will not start. Each job is a
.IR subcommand ,
named as the first argument.
.B vlhe
with no subcommand prints its usage and exits.
.PP
.B vlhe.gtk
is the control centre, a GTK 1.2 program in the style of the
desktop's own control panels. Run with no arguments, or with only the
options below, it opens its window. Its first argument may also be one
of the subcommands below, in which case it behaves exactly as
.B vlhe
does and opens no window; the two programs share that code.
.PP
Some things can be done only in the control centre: attaching a disc
image to a virtual drive, choosing which drive
.I /dev/cdrom
reaches, setting per-program levels by program rather than by
channel, the CD+G viewer, Render MIDI, and the File menu's Save,
Load, Reset and Restore of the configuration. The control centre's
own help text is also kept as a plain text file,
.IR /usr/local/share/vlhe/vlhe-help.txt
.RI ( /usr/share/vlhe/vlhe-help.txt
from the package).
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B volume
With no further arguments, report every vsound channel: its number,
the program using it, its level as a bar and a percentage, and
whether it is muted or is the channel reserved for the MIDI synth.
A free channel shows as
.BR (free) .
A muted channel shows its level, not 0: mute is kept separately from
the level.
.IP
These levels set programs against each other within VLHE's own mix.
They are not the sound card's output level; for overall loudness use
the card's own mixer.
.TP
.BI "volume " "N LEVEL"
Set channel
.I N
to
.I LEVEL
per cent, 0 to 200. 100 is unchanged; above 100 boosts a quiet
program, and the mixer's limiter keeps the sum from clipping. Setting
a level on a muted channel leaves it muted, at the new level.
.TP
.BI "volume " N " mute" "\fR|\fPunmute"
Mute or unmute channel
.IR N .
.IP
Channel numbers are reused: when a program exits, the next one to
open takes its slot. If the program on a channel changes between
reading it and setting it,
.B vlhe
says so rather than setting the new program's level.
.TP
.B apply
Load the kernel modules and start the daemons, as the configuration
describes. Needs root. It first prints the plan - every command, in
order - and then runs it. If a required step fails, it stops and
names the step; the steps before it have already run, and
.B vlhe apply \-u
takes down what is up.
.IP
What it loads is each component's
.B LoadAtBoot
setting in
.IR /etc/vlhe.conf ,
the same setting the boot script uses. The control centre's
"Include in load" boxes do not affect it.
.IP
It refuses to run on any kernel other than the 2.2 kernel the
modules were built for, and there is deliberately no way to override
that. With no configuration file it plans from the defaults.
.IP
Before changing anything it compares the device files it manages,
such as
.IR /dev/dsp ,
with the machine's baseline - how they were before VLHE first changed
them. If one has been changed since by something else, what happens is
.B [Load] BaselineDrift
in
.BR vlhe.conf (5):
by default it asks on the terminal whether to undo the change, accept
it as the new baseline, load anyway or cancel. With no terminal to
ask, it keeps the baseline, loads and says so.
.TP
.B status
Report which configuration files are in use, whether each module is
loaded, and whether each daemon is running, with its process ID.
Needs no root. This reads the machine itself
.RI ( /proc/modules
and the daemons' process ID files), so a daemon that has died is
reported as not running even if its process ID file remains.
It also lists anything that
.B needs attention
\(em a
.I /dev/dsp
link that may be left over from an earlier load and cannot be shown to
be, or a
.I /dev/cdrom
left pointing at a target of VLHE's own that is gone \(em every time
until it is put right.
.TP
.B repair
Ask about each thing that needs attention, one at a time: for a
.I /dev/dsp
link, put back the stock device node or keep it as it is; for
.IR /dev/cdrom ,
point it at one of the CD drives found, or remove the link. Nothing is
changed without an answer, and it refuses to run without a terminal.
The choice becomes the machine's baseline, so it is not asked again.
Changing a device node needs root and the same 2.2.16 kernel as
.BR apply .
.TP
.B setup
Run
.BR setup-vlhe (8),
the menu-driven configurator that writes
.IR /etc/vlhe.conf .
Root only; it refuses at once for anyone else, rather than after the
menus have been answered. It runs the
.B setup-vlhe
installed beside
.B vlhe
if there is one (and it belongs to root or to the caller and nobody
else can write it), then
.IR /usr/sbin/setup-vlhe ,
then
.IR /usr/local/sbin/setup-vlhe .
It takes no arguments; to write a different file, run
.B setup-vlhe \-f
.I FILE
directly.
.PP
Planned and not built:
.BI "vlhe attach " "N IMAGE" \fR,
to put a disc image in drive
.IR N .
The usage message lists it so that a user who has read about it is
told it is not there yet.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS vlhe volume
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-plain
Print one line per channel, tab-separated, for scripts: the channel
number, the program name, the level, and
.B on
or
.BR muted .
A free channel prints
.B \-
in the last three columns.
.SS vlhe apply
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Print what would be done and run nothing. The kernel check still
runs, so running this on the wrong machine says so here rather than
later.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-unload
Stop the daemons and unload the modules instead, putting
.I /dev/dsp
and
.I /dev/cdrom
back as they were. Acts on what is loaded, not on the settings. It
also finishes a load that was never unloaded - after a power loss or
a crash - by putting back what that load recorded changing.
.TP
.B \-\-boot
How the init script loads. If a load was never unloaded, finish it
first - only what it recorded changing, never a path someone has
changed since - and load only if that worked; with
.B [Boot] FinishLeftover
set to 0 the boot only notes it and does not load. What the boot did
is shown once, the next time
.B vlhe status
or the control centre's Status page is opened. Without this option,
a load that finds such a leftover refuses and says how to finish it.
.SS vlhe.gtk
.TP
.B \-\-reset
Clear the control centre's saved interface font and start with
GTK's default, and save that at once. This is the way back from a
font chosen in File > Preferences that is too small to read.
.TP
.BI \-m " PAGE"
Open on the page whose name begins with
.IR PAGE ,
ignoring case: for example
.B \-m volume
or
.BR "\-m cd+g" .
The page names are listed under PAGES.
.TP
.BI \-T " N"
Open the starting page on its tab number
.IR N ,
counting from 0.
.TP
.B \-V
Draw the Volume page's faders vertically for this run, whatever the
saved setting says.
.TP
.BI \-F " FONT"
Use the X font
.I FONT
for every widget, for this run only. Not a setting and not saved; it
exists for looking at a font before choosing one. A name containing
a quote or a backslash is refused.
.TP
.B \-\-reset\-dialog
Shows the Reset Settings dialog on start.
.TP
.B \-\-review\-dialog
Shows the configuration review on start.
.IP
Both are test aids for the control centre's development build, which runs
against scripted data rather than the machine, and have no use on a
real machine.
.PP
GTK's own options, such as
.BI \-\-display " DISPLAY"\fR,
are also accepted. An option
.B vlhe.gtk
does not recognise is ignored.
.SH PAGES
The control centre's sidebar holds seven pages:
.BR Status ,
.BR Volume ,
.BR "Sound Settings" ,
.BR "Midi Settings" ,
.BR "CD Settings" ,
.B "CD+G Viewer"
and
.BR "Render MIDI" .
.PP
On the settings pages, OK and Apply hold changes in memory for the
session - Load, Restart and Render use them straight away - and only
File > Save Configuration writes them to disk. Quitting with unsaved
changes asks first. Writing
.I /etc/vlhe.conf
needs root; when an ordinary user saves, the machine settings go to
a draft file which root can bring in with File > Load
Configuration.
.PP
The control centre is driven by the mouse. It defines no keyboard
shortcuts beyond GTK 1.2's ordinary Tab movement between controls.
GTK 1.2 has no accessibility layer, so a screen reader cannot read
the window;
.I vlhe-help.txt
can be read in a terminal, and
.B vlhe
is the readable route to what it covers.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success. For
.BR status ,
every module is loaded and every daemon is running. For
.BR "apply \-n" ,
the plan was printed.
.TP
.B 1
The command could not be carried out: for
.BR volume ,
vsound is not loaded, there is no such channel, it is not in use, or
the program on it has just exited; for
.BR apply ,
it refused to run on this kernel, or a step failed; for
.BR status ,
at least one module or daemon is down, or there was nothing to
report; for
.BR repair ,
a change could not be made; for
.BR setup ,
the caller is not root or
.B setup-vlhe
could not be run. Also
.BR vlhe.gtk ,
when its backend could not start.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error: no subcommand, an unknown subcommand, or a bad
argument. Also
.B repair
run without a terminal.
.PP
.B vlhe setup
otherwise exits with the status of
.BR setup-vlhe .
.B vlhe.gtk
exits 0 when its window is closed.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HOME
Locates the user's own settings,
.IR ~/.vlhe/vlhe.conf ,
the extra SoundFont folder
.IR ~/.vlhe/sf2 ,
and the
.I ~/.cddb
folder searched for CD titles.
.TP
.B DISPLAY
The X display
.B vlhe.gtk
opens. The subcommands do not use it.
.SH FILES
An installed VLHE uses these; a portable VLHE (built with
.BR "make portable" )
keeps the same files inside its own folder instead and touches nothing
outside it. Which one a program is was decided when it was built; the
.I PORTABLE
file in a portable folder only says what the folder is.
.TP
.I /etc/vlhe.conf
The machine's settings. See
.BR vlhe.conf (5).
.TP
.I ~/.vlhe/vlhe.conf
Each user's own settings: the interface, the CD+G viewer, Render
MIDI, the SoundFonts.
.TP
.I ~/.vlhe/vlhe-draft.conf
An ordinary user's saved machine settings, waiting for root to load
them. Written beside the user's own settings file.
.TP
.I /etc/vlhe/defaults.conf
The settings
.B setup-vlhe
first wrote. Used if
.I /etc/vlhe.conf
is lost, and as the defaults for File > Reset Settings.
.TP
.I /var/lib/vlhe/state/drives
Which image is in each virtual drive, and which come back at the next
load.
.TP
.I /var/lib/vlhe/mixers
The sound cards' mixer levels, when Save and restore the sound card's
mixer levels is on.
.TP
.I /var/lib/vlhe/volumes
Each program's level, when Keep each program's level across restarts
is on.
.TP
.I /var/lib/vlhe/vlhe-session
What the last load brought up, so that an unload takes down exactly
that.
.TP
.I /var/lib/vlhe/sessions/
Each load that was unloaded cleanly, filed under the time it ended,
so a past load can be read back.
.TP
.IR /var/lib/vlhe/vlhe-baseline- machine
How the device files VLHE manages were before it first changed them,
one file per machine (its host name and root filesystem).
.TP
.IR /var/lib/vlhe/dsp.was ", " /var/lib/vlhe/cdrom.was
What
.I /dev/dsp
and
.I /dev/cdrom
were before VLHE redirected them, so unloading can put them back.
.TP
.I /var/log/vlhe/DAEMON.LOG
The daemons' output.
.TP
.I /var/log/vlhe/changes
The journal: every change a load, an unload or a repair made to the
machine, and how it ended. Messages that say "the full account is
in" name this file.
.TP
.I /var/run/vlhe/ctl/
The daemons' process ID files and control channels.
.TP
.IR ~/.cddb ", " /root/.cddb ", " /usr/X11R6/share/apps/kscd/cddb
Searched, in that order, for a CD's titles when it carries no
CD-TEXT - the cache KsCD and grip write.
.TP
.I /usr/local/share/vlhe/vlhe-init
The boot script. INSTALL says how to link it in.
.TP
.I /usr/local/share/vlhe/vlhe-help.txt
The control centre's help, page by page, as plain text.
.SH EXAMPLES
See what is loaded and running:
.PP
.RS
vlhe status
.RE
.PP
Show what loading would do, then do it (as root):
.PP
.RS
vlhe apply \-n
.br
vlhe apply
.RE
.PP
Halve channel 1's level, then mute it:
.PP
.RS
vlhe volume 1 50
.br
vlhe volume 1 mute
.RE
.PP
Recover from an unreadable font:
.PP
.RS
vlhe.gtk \-\-reset
.RE
.SH BUGS
Load and Unload in the control centre stop the window from
responding until they finish. A step that hangs is stopped after 30
seconds.
.PP
A level cannot yet be set for a program that is not running, and
.B vlhe attach
is not built.
